-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Data sources
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

OSV.dev (https://osv.dev): vulnerability advisories, queried with package names and versions.
OSV aggregates databases that keep their own licenses, including the GitHub Advisory Database
(CC BY 4.0), the Python Packaging Advisory Database (CC BY 4.0), the Go Vulnerability Database
(CC BY 4.0) and the RustSec Advisory Database (CC0 1.0). osv-scanner may also query deps.dev
(https://deps.dev) to resolve versions in requirements.txt files.

CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities Catalog (https://www.cisa.gov/known-exploited-vulnerabilities-catalog):
which vulnerabilities are being exploited in the wild.

EPSS, Exploit Prediction Scoring System, by FIRST (https://www.first.org/epss): the probability
that a vulnerability is exploited in the next 30 days.

Have I Been Pwned (https://haveibeenpwned.com): list of publicly known data breaches, used to flag
keys for services that had a recent breach. Licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
International (CC BY 4.0).

endoflife.date (https://endoflife.date): release and end-of-life dates for language runtimes.
Data licensed under the MIT License.

GitHub repository security advisories (https://docs.github.com/rest/security-advisories): advisories
for installed AI coding tools.
